            <ArticleTitle>'The eyes see what the mind knows.' Adult-onset Still's disease, a case series and review in a south Asian population.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e96-100</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1756-185X.2012.01812.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Adult-onset Still's disease (AOSD) is a rare disease. Very few cases have been reported from the South-Asian region so the aim of this study is to assess the clinical and laboratory aspects of 15 patients with AOSD in a tertiary referral hospital in Karachi.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective data was collected from all patients diagnosed using Yamaguchi criteria for AOSD between January 2004 and December 2010 at Jinnah Medical College Hospital, Karachi.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Data of 15 patients with AOSD were analyzed. Their ages ranged from 17 to 55 years, the male-to-female ratio being 6:1. The most common clinical features were fever and articular symptoms (100%), sore throat (60%), rash (53.3%), weight loss (93.3%), lymphadenopathy (40%) and elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (86.7%). All patients had leukocytosis with counts&gt;20,000/mm 3 were seen in 40%. Elevated liver enzymes were present in 80% of the case series and hyperferritinemia in 100% with a mean of 3,962 ng/mL (range 555-13,865). Ambiguity in presentation and lack of serologic markers make diagnosis of AOSD difficult as 40% of patients were receiving empirical anti-tuberculous therapy prior to final diagnosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is necessary for physicians to have a high index of suspicion for AOSD in patients with high-grade fever, arthralgia and leukocytosis.</AbstractText>
